14.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: I’m still on H. The F case is mathematically sound: ΔAΔB unmasks a 2000/ml xy-dependent restriction, and ΔC gives a 2000/ml xy-independent drop. But that is not the same as F being the best answer. The key genetic result is redundancy: ΔA alone and ΔB alone are both 5000/ml in wtL and -xyL, so neither single deletion unmasks the phenotype. Only ΔAΔB does. Thus the supported statement is “A and B redundantly deactivate the xy product,” not “B deactivates the product of xy” with A omitted. If B were the answer-level xy deactivator, ΔB alone should have lowered wtL; it did not.

The F supporters keep adding “and symmetrically A does the same” to make F acceptable. That is the tell: the complete mechanism is:

xy influences infection;  
A and B redundantly deactivate xy;  
C acts independently of xy.

None of A–G states that. F is a true fragment but asymmetric. Its second clause is also too broad: C being xy-independent does not establish that C shares no host proteins with A generally; the experiment only maps the xy-dependent axis. So “no false component” is a weaker standard than “states the correct mechanism.” D is unsupported, and A/B/C/E/G misassign C to xy or deny xy’s role. H remains the safest.

